**Hariyali** (2003) was a watershed (**water management**) programme through panchayats. **Vishesh Krishi and Gram Udyog Yojana** (2004) promoted **agricultural exports**. The **Digital India Land Records Modernization Programme** (NLRMP, 2008) computerises land records; **SVAMITVA** (2020) maps village homes by drones.

@section 4.5 | Marketing and Prices

@q 3:80-83

@table
Body / law | Role
**NAFED** (1958) | **Apex cooperative** for agricultural **marketing** and export; price support purchases
**APMC Acts** (state laws) | **Regulate agricultural markets** (mandis)
**e-NAM** (2016) | Online national agricultural market
IFFCO | Fertiliser cooperative, not an export agency
STC, MMTC | Public trading corporations
@end

Demand for farm products is **price-inelastic**: a bumper crop lowers prices sharply and can cut farm income.

@q 3:92

@q 3:94-100

@src1 3:10
@one
Which of the following recommends the Minimum Support Price for crops?
@ans Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices
@end

@table
Price term | Meaning
**MSP** | Floor price announced for **22 crops** (plus FRP for sugarcane) before sowing, on **CACP** advice, approved by the **CCEA**; since 2018 at least **1.5 times** the A2+FL cost
**CACP** | Set up in **1965** as the **Agricultural Prices Commission**; renamed 1985
**FRP** | **Fair and Remunerative Price** of **sugarcane** (since 2009), approved by the **CCEA**
**Procurement price** | Price at which the government actually **buys** grain
**Price Stabilisation Fund** | 2015; buffer stocks of **onion, potato** and pulses
@end

MSP is a safety net: if market prices are **higher**, farmers sell in the market, not to the government, and it does not guarantee "immense profit". Recent MSPs: paddy (common) **Rs. 2,369** per quintal (2025-26), wheat **Rs. 2,425** (2025-26 marketing season). **Kerala** (2020) was the first state to fix MSPs for **vegetables and fruits**.

@q 3:110

@section 4.6 | Agricultural Credit

@q 3:101-107

@q 3:108-109

@table
Credit fact | Detail
Largest source of farm credit | **Commercial banks** (about 75-80%), then cooperatives, RRBs
Smallest | **Foreign banks**
Long-term credit | **Land Development Banks** (now Agricultural and Rural Development Banks)
**Kisan Credit Card** | **1998-99** (NABARD, R.V. Gupta committee); short-term crop loans; extended to fisheries and animal husbandry
**Interest subvention** | Loans at 7%; **3% prompt-repayment** incentive → effective **4%**
Order of creation | **RRBs (1975) → NABARD (1982) → SHG-bank linkage (1992) → KCC (1998)**
@end

Working (running) capital is seed, fertiliser, irrigation water and labour; **land revenue** is a fixed charge. Moneylending at high interest drains the rural economy.

@trap
NFSA = **75% rural, 50% urban**, 5 kg; in force **2013**. **NFSM** began rabi 2007-08. **Operation Flood = milk (Kurien)**; **blue = fish, yellow = oilseeds, golden = horticulture, grey = fertilisers, black = petroleum.** **First KVK 1974, Puducherry.** **PMFBY 2016**; first crop insurance 1985. **CACP** recommends MSP; **CCEA** approves MSP and FRP. **Commercial banks** give the most farm credit. **KCC 1998-99.** RRB → NABARD → SHG → KCC.
@end

